  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "As partial pressure of a gas in a mixture is the pressure it would exert for the same volume and temperature, if it alone occupied the vessel, therefore, for common <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are partial pressures<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>RT</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>RT</mi></math>&nbsp;<br /><br />Here, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> refer to neon gas and oxygen gas respectively<br /><br />Now, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>⇒</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;( given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>)<br />If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are number of molecules of two gases, then<br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></mfrac></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></msub></mfrac></math>",
